column2:
No radiographic evidence of pulmonary metastases.  However, please note that
 if there is persistent clinical concern for metastatic disease,
 cross-sectional imaging should be obtained.

No acute cardiopulmonary process.  No displaced rib fracture is identified;
 however, if high clinical concern for rib fracture persists, dedicated rib
 series or CT is more sensitive.

1. Improved mild pulmonary edema.  
 2. Consolidation within the retrocardiac region may represent atelectasis;
 however, pneumonia cannot be excluded in the correct clinical setting.

1.  Subtle opacity in the right lower lobe likely represents atelectasis;
 however, may represent pneumonia in the correct clinical setting.

 2.  Mild left basilar atelectasis.

Lingular opacity may represent atelectasis, but potentially pneumonia or even
 aspiration in the appropriate setting.  Chronicity is also uncertain;
 correlation with prior radiographs may prove helpful in determining acuity.
